  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"117\" data-end=\"236\">The <strong data-start=\"121\" data-end=\"147\">Urine Copper Spot Test</strong> measures the concentration of copper in a random urine sample. It is typically used for:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"238\" data-end=\"477\">\n<li data-start=\"238\" data-end=\"333\">\n<p data-start=\"240\" data-end=\"333\"><strong data-start=\"240\" data-end=\"284\">Screening or monitoring Wilson's disease</strong> (a rare inherited disorder of copper metabolism)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"334\" data-end=\"380\">\n<p data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"380\"><strong data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"380\">Evaluating copper overload or deficiency</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"381\" data-end=\"415\">\n<p data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"415\"><strong data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"415\">Monitoring chelation therapy</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"416\" data-end=\"477\">\n<p data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"477\"><strong data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"477\">Assessing occupational or environmental copper exposure</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"479\" data-end=\"625\">Since it's a <strong data-start=\"492\" data-end=\"505\">spot test</strong>, results may be adjusted for creatinine (e.g., copper/creatinine ratio) to account for urine concentration variability.</p>\n<p data-start=\"479\" data-end=\"625\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wilson&rsquo;s disease</strong></td>\n<td>Defective copper excretion by the liver leads to accumulation and excess copper in urine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute or chronic hepatitis</strong></td>\n<td>Liver inflammation can cause increased copper release and excretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Biliary cirrhosis</strong></td>\n<td>Cholestatic liver disease increases copper retention and urinary excretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Copper toxicity</strong></td>\n<td>Excess copper ingestion (dietary, supplement, or occupational exposure) leads to elevated levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chelation therapy</strong></td>\n<td>When patients are treated for copper overload (e.g., with penicillamine), copper levels in urine may rise due to increased excretion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"479\" data-end=\"625\">&nbsp;</p>",
